<title>BELGIUM: EU adopts ban on pig and poultry drug avoparcin.</title>
<headline>EU adopts ban on pig and poultry drug avoparcin.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1997-01-30</dateline>
<text>
<p>The European Commission adopted a European Union ban on the drug avoparcin on Thursday after the two British Commissioners withdrew their objections, a senior official said.</p>
<p>&quot;The two British Commissioners made their point but have now agreed to the proposal which is adopted by written proceedure,&quot; the official said.</p>
<p>Avoparcin, manufactured by Roche UK, part of Roche Holdings AG, is used as a growth enhancer by pig and poultry producers.</p>
<p>Farm Commissioner Franz Fischler proposed a ban on the drug, which is not permitted in Denmark and Germany, in December after the EU standing veterinary committee supported concerns that it could cause resistance to antibiotics in humans.</p>
<p>Both Britain's commissioners Sir Leon Brittan and Neil Kinnock blocked adoption of the ban by written procedure last week, asking for more scientific evidence to support the proposal.</p>
<p>-- Brussels Newsroom 32 2 287 6800</p>
</text>
